.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt EXPR 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm expr
.Nd evaluate an expression
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar expression
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
evaluates
.Ar expression
and writes the result to the standard output.
Each operator and operand must be a separate argument, and characters special to
the shell must be quoted.
.Pp
An integer is an optional
.Ql -
followed by one or more decimal digits; arithmetic is done on 64-bit signed
values.
The operators, from the lowest precedence to the highest, are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar expr1 Li \&| Ar expr2
.Ar expr1
if it is neither the null string nor zero; otherwise
.Ar expr2
if it is neither; otherwise 0.
.It Ar expr1 Li \&& Ar expr2
.Ar expr1
if neither expression is null or zero; otherwise 0.
.It Ar expr1 Bro Li = , > , >= , < , <= , != Brc Ar expr2
1 if the relation holds, 0 if not.
When both operands are integers they are compared as numbers; otherwise as
strings, byte by byte.
.It Ar expr1 Bro Li + , - Brc Ar expr2
Addition and subtraction.
.It Ar expr1 Bro Li * , / , % Brc Ar expr2
Multiplication, division (truncating toward zero) and remainder.
.It Ar expr1 Li \&: Ar expr2
Matches
.Ar expr1
against the basic regular expression
.Ar expr2 ,
anchored at the start of
.Ar expr1 .
If the expression contains a subexpression
.Li \e( Ns ... Ns Li \e) ,
the result is the text the first one matched, or the null string; otherwise it
is the number of bytes matched.
.It Li \&( Ar expr Li \&)
Grouping.
.El
.Pp
A leading
.Ql --
argument is skipped.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The result is neither null nor zero.
.It 1
The result is null or zero.
.It 2
The expression is invalid: a syntax error, an operand of arithmetic that is not
an integer, division by zero, overflow, or a bad regular expression.
.It 3
The result could not be written.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Increment a shell variable:
.Dl $ i=$(expr $i + 1)
.Pp
The last component of a path name:
.Dl $ expr //usr/lib/libc.a : '.*/\e(.*\e)'
.Pp
Whether a string is all digits (prints its length if so):
.Dl $ expr "$s" : '[0-9]*$'
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Xr test 1 ,
.Xr re_format 7
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq).
Overflow, which POSIX leaves undefined, is an error, as in the BSDs.
.Sh HISTORY
An
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v7 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
.Sh CAVEATS
An operand that looks like an operator is taken as one where an operator can
stand:
.Dl $ expr "$a" = "$b"
fails when
.Va a
is
.Ql \&( .
Prefixing both operands with a character avoids it:
.Dl $ expr "x$a" = "x$b"
